[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I didn\'t edit a single shot in this video. I didn\'t make the B-roll, and I didn\'t build the animation, either. I just taught Claude a few viral editing styles, and it cut the whole thing for me. Now, you\'ve probably seen AI try to edit videos before, but it\'s the same result every time. The cuts are awkward, the captions are full of errors, and the'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'B-roll doesn\'t always correlate with what\'s being spoken about in your footage. But, here\'s [music] the thing. The difference between a generic AI edit and one that looks like it came from a real editor doesn\'t come down to the tool we use. It comes down to the style we teach it. And the workflow I\'m about'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to show you allows Claude to reach into your footage and edit [music] it through a connection with Higgsfield. If you\'re wondering why I use Higgsfield, it\'s the one platform that gives Claude every editing tool and every model it needs to actually build the video.'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, today, the plan is simple. We\'ll hand Claude our own footage, teach it any style we want, and have it cut different viral videos for us. But, before we can edit any videos, we need two things: the Claude desktop app and a'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quick connection to Higgsfield. Now, it\'s important to remember that the desktop app looks almost identical to Claude in your browser, but with one difference that matters here. The app version can actually reach the video files on our computer. So, first, we\'ll'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'download the desktop app and open it up. Once it\'s open, we\'ll see a chat and a co-work toggle right in the message box. We want co-work. It\'s the one that can open our footage, call on the tools we\'ve connected, and do the actual work for us. But, on its own, co-work can\'t'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'edit a single video yet. And that\'s what the Higgsfield MCP is for. Think of it like a direct line between Claude and Higgsfield, one that lets Claude reach every model and every editing tool Higgsfield has. So, we\'ll head into settings, click connectors, then select'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'add custom connector. We\'ll name it Higgsfield, then paste the connection link into the box. After that, we\'ll hit connect, and a browser window will pop up asking us to sign into our Higgsfield account. Once we enter our credentials, it\'ll drop us right back into Claude.'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But, to prove it\'s actually listening, let\'s give it our first clip and see what it does. This is a raw street interview, just a guy stopping someone on the street with no cuts or captions. And for our first try, we\'ll prompt it'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the way most people would. Let\'s drop the video in and give Claude one vague instruction, telling it to edit our video into an engaging short for social media. And straight away, we hit a problem. Claude doesn\'t botch it exactly, but it does something more'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'telling. It stops and asks us a couple of questions. How should it structure this video? What should the captions look like? Question after question, because we never told it exactly what we\'re going for. And when we let Claude make those decisions on its own, this is what comes back.'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Excuse me, sorry to stop you. If you could relive one moment from the last year, what would it be? &gt;&gt; Oh, that\'s easy. It was my birthday. I got to spend the whole day with my'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'favorite people. &gt;&gt; That\'s great. Thanks so much. &gt;&gt; Now, it\'s not bad. It cut the footage, captioned the whole thing, and honestly, it looks pretty decent. But watch the pacing. There are moments of dead air and a few pauses that a scroll-stopping short just can\'t afford. On social'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'media, that could be the difference between someone watching and someone swiping away. All in all, it made an okay cut, but it didn\'t make a tight one. Because a vague instruction will always produce a generic edit. So, here\'s what we\'ll do instead. Instead of describing what we want every single'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time, we\'ll teach Claude once by handing it an editing style document. Think of it as a style guide we would use to brief an editor. It\'ll provide instructions on how fast to cut, exactly what the captions should look like, and even tell it where a graphic should earn its place. All editing style documents'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
